                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "An entertaining game with a lot of variety in it. The main thing you spend your time doing is flying a spaceship in an overheard, 2d gameplay style.\n\nThere is a open galaxy to explore, made up of quite a few systems or gravity wells. Basically everything is in the systems, but the task of navigating to each system is more interesting than just waiting for the travel time to end. There are damaging nebula all over the map. And the path to a system might mean you must navigate around the nebula. You start off with a fuzzy map that basically shows the nebula position, but you collect map pieces that shows more precisely where the nebulae lay. So you might be able to find shorter paths through gaps in the nebulae that aren’t obvious from the basic map.\n\nThere are many ships to try, and many upgrades to buy. The game looks after you, because buying ships and upgrades are not important decisions that can break your finances. Every ship you buy can be resold for what looks like atleast 90% of its value. So choosing a ship doesn’t lock you in. Upgrades are even better. Not only do upgrades transfer easily between each ship, but they also seem to hold their full value. As in, if you buy an upgrade, and then decide to sell it, you get the full money back. So you can mix and match, and try upgrades out without any worry about wasting money. Getting money isn’t painful, it seems to have a decent balance, in that it takes some effort to get money and buy better equipment, but it’s not a big chore that takes a long time.\n\nSpace Combat is interesting, as you have momentum, and have some extra abilities, like boosters and missiles. But it can be a bit difficult to tell what is happening sometimes.\nYou can hunt pirates down. And you can mine asteroids.\n\n\n\nDespite being a space ship game, there are some other types of gameplay. You can explore wrecked ships for salvage. You can walk on stations and talk to people. You can fight sometimes on stations sometimes.  There is enough variety to give you a change of pace when you feel like it.\n\nThe game is centred around the story. But the story also doesn’t really lock you off at any point, so you can just do whatever nearly all of the time. The story does a decent job of being interesting to continue through to the end.\n\nOverall an interesting game that is fun. It provides a lot more variety than you might expect. A bit of an open world space sim, on a smaller scale. The focus on the story gives a bit more structure than you might usually get in this type of game, and is perfect for the smaller scale, but still open and large enough, universe.",
